mode: "100644"
text: "# Platform Playbook\n\nHashtags do a different job on every platform, and the counts differ. Never paste one block\neverywhere. Match the approach to where the audience actually is.\n\n> Counts, caps, and behaviors are current 2026 norms — re-verify quarterly; platforms change these\n> without much notice.\n\n## Instagram\n\n- **Hard cap: 5 hashtags** (enforced, rolled out Dec 2025). More are blocked or trimmed.\n- Use **~3–5 precise, relevant** tags — niche/topic/community/location/branded, not generic.\n- **Mid-tier tags (~10K–500K posts)** beat mega-tags — you can actually surface there.\n- **Placement: in the caption** (indexed immediately for search). Some large accounts use the first\n  comment for a clean caption; for most, caption is best for SEO.\n- **Hashtags follow removed (Dec 2024)** — tags no longer feed subscribers; they're search/category\n  signals only.\n- **Reels:** lean on **trending audio + keywords**; hashtags just categorize. Audio often does more\n  for reach than tags.\n- **The real lever:** keyword-rich caption + on-screen text (Instagram indexes both).\n\n## TikTok\n\n- **~3–5 hashtags**, and **treat them as keywords**: `#BestBudgetLaptop`, not `#Tech`. One broad\n  category tag + niche tags that match real searches.\n- **TikTok is a search engine** (~half of Gen Z start product research there). The ranking move is\n  the **triple mention** — primary keyword **spoken + on-screen + in the caption** (front).\n- **#fyp / #viral are useless** — they don't affect FYP placement.\n- **Avoid the 30-tag spray** — TikTok deprioritizes it.\n- Optional SEO tactic: a **pinned keyword-rich comment** acts like a second caption.\n\n## LinkedIn\n\n- **~3–5 specific** hashtags, treated as a **lightweight topic signal, not a reach driver**.\n  LinkedIn has deprioritized hashtag distribution (its own product lead calls them \"nice to have,\n  not need to have\"); its algorithm reads semantic meaning from the post body directly.\n- So the lever is **keywords in the post body**; if you tag, specific beats broad\n  (`#SaaSLeadership` > `#Leadership`) — never a broad-tag pile.\n\n## X / Twitter\n\n- **0–2, often zero.** X has **deprioritized hashtags** (even called them clutter in ads); more than\n  one or two can *reduce* engagement.\n- Use a single tag only to **join a specific conversation or event** (a conference, a live moment).\n\n## Threads\n\n- **One topic tag per post** (Threads' model). Keep it relevant; don't force it.\n\n## Pinterest\n\n- **Keywords in the title and description are the lever** (Pinterest is a visual search engine).\n  Hashtags are minor/deprecated — don't rely on them.\n\n## YouTube\n\n- **Keywords in the title and description** drive search. A few relevant tags/hashtags help; the\n  first ~3 hashtags can show above the title. Don't stuff.\n\n## Facebook\n\n- Minimal. **1–2** relevant tags at most; hashtags do little for reach here.\n\n## Quick reference\n\n| Platform | Hashtags | Real lever |\n|---|---|---|\n| Instagram | 3–5 (hard cap 5) | keyword caption + on-screen text; trending audio (Reels) |\n| TikTok | 3–5, as keywords | triple-mention keyword (spoken+on-screen+caption) |\n| LinkedIn | 3–5 specific (light signal) | keywords in the body; tags don't drive reach |\n| X/Twitter | 0–2 | the post itself; tag only to join a moment |\n| Threads | 1 topic tag | the post + topic tag |\n| Pinterest | minor | keywords in title/description |\n| YouTube | a few | keywords in title/description |\n| Facebook | 1–2 | the post itself |\n"
